  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a6334982dcae08e4698af5d208c769f4, type: 3}
  m_Name: Question3_StarryNight
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  description:
  - Technically, van Gogh used oil on canvas with heavy impasto, meaning thick textured
    paint that creates visible ridges and gives the surface a physical presence.
    The brush strokes in the sky flow in broad swirling arcs, as though the sky itself
    is in motion. He selected intense blues such as ultramarine and cobalt for the
    sky, and brilliant yellows like indian yellow and zinc yellow for the stars and
    moon. The contrast between deep blue and bright yellow or orange intensifies
    the visual effect and renders the night scene with heightened emotion. He was
    not attempting a purely naturalistic image. He exaggerated form and color to
    express feeling.
  awnsers:
  - {fileID: 8300000, guid: aff5586446258414692d4fb55cb3cb2d, type: 3}
